\chapter{The PEG Definition Grammar: Rules and
Channels}\label{ch27-the-peg-definition-grammar-rules-and-channels}

The PEG (Parsing Expression Grammar) engine is a lightweight, additive
grammar subsystem layered on top of the pattern-matching and
parser-combinator machinery introduced earlier in this manual. Where
Chapter 9 described the compile-time
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
regex engine, and Chapters 23 through 25 built parsers from small
composable functions, the PEG engine provides a container-oriented
model: a grammar is a set of named rules, each carrying a compile-time
pattern and a semantic action, evaluated under PEG ordered-choice
semantics. This chapter describes the grammar container and its rules.
The matcher machinery that consumes a grammar streamingly is the subject
of Chapter 28.

The PEG subsystem is deliberately additive. No existing type, function,
or layout in \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} is modified to accommodate
it; the entire engine lives in a single self-contained section of the
header behind forward-declared structs (\texttt{PEGDefinition},
\texttt{PEGRule}, \texttt{PEGMatch}, \texttt{PEGParseResult},
\texttt{PEGMatcher}). A grammar can be constructed, populated with
rules, and used to parse input without pulling in any of the combinator
types from Chapter 23. Readers familiar with the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern} regex subset will find
the pattern language identical, because the PEG engine compiles its rule
patterns with the same runtime matcher described in Chapter 9.

This chapter covers the factory
\texttt{create\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}definition(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
the \texttt{PEGDefinition} container, the
\texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule\textless{}\allowbreak{}Pattern\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}action)\allowbreak{}}
template, the \texttt{PEGRule} record, the channel abstraction
(\texttt{PEGChannel}, \texttt{PEGIgnoreChannel\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{PEGDefaultChanne\allowbreak{}l},
\texttt{new\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}channel}), the
\texttt{PEGMatch} value handed to semantic actions, the
\texttt{PEGParseResult} returned by
\texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, and the ordered-choice
algorithm that drives rule selection. Worked examples progress from a
trivial whitespace-skipping tokenizer to a small expression grammar.
Cross-references to Chapter 4 (FixedString), Chapter 9 (pattern subset),
Chapter 10 (ASTNode), and Chapters 23 through 25 (parser combinators)
appear throughout.

\section{The PEG Model in MuDSL}\label{ch27-the-peg-model-in-mudsl}

A Parsing Expression Grammar, in the formal sense, is an ordered set of
production rules where each rule is a parsing expression and choice is
ordered: the first alternative that matches wins, and later alternatives
are not tried at the same position. The MuDSL PEG engine embodies that
semantics directly. A \texttt{PEGDefinition} stores rules in declaration
order, and \texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} walks the input
position by position, attempting each visible rule at the current offset
until one succeeds. There is no backtracking across positions, no
ambiguity, and no lookahead beyond what the pattern subset itself
provides.

This makes the engine a scanner rather than a recursive-descent parser
of nested structures. Each rule consumes a contiguous span of input at
the current position, fires its semantic action, and advances the
position. The grammar does not, on its own, build a recursive tree; that
is the job of the semantic action, which may use the AST facilities of
Chapter 10 and Chapter 11 to construct a tree, or simply emit tokens.
The PEG engine's responsibility is narrower: decide which rule matches
here, run its action, advance.

Because the engine is layered on the
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern} regex subset, every rule
pattern is a flat sequence of atoms and quantifiers. There is no
alternation operator \texttt{|}, no parenthesised grouping, and no
backreferences. Composition of alternatives is expressed at the rule
level, by declaring several rules in order, not within a single pattern
string. This restriction is a deliberate consequence of reusing the
Chapter 9 matcher and keeps rule patterns simple to read and cheap to
compile.

\section{Creating a Grammar}\label{ch27-creating-a-grammar}

A grammar is created with the free function
\texttt{create\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}definition(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
which returns a default-constructed \texttt{PEGDefinition} by value. The
definition owns its rules in an internal
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}PEGRule\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and returns stable references to them as long as rules are only appended
and the definition itself remains alive. Moving a \texttt{PEGDefinition}
is supported by the implicit move constructor of its members, but
references obtained before a move must not be used after the move.

\begin{code}
auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition();
\end{code}

The returned object has its \texttt{parent} pointer set to
\texttt{nullptr}, marking it as a root grammar. Root grammars are the
usual starting point; derived grammars, created with
\texttt{derive\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}definition(\allowbreak{}parent)\allowbreak{}},
are discussed later in this chapter. From the moment of creation the
grammar contains no rules, and a call to
\texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} on an empty grammar will
simply fail to match the first character.

The factory is intentionally trivial. It exists so that user code does
not need to name the \texttt{PEGDefinition} constructor or worry about
default member values; it also makes the intent at the call site
explicit. The grammar is a value type, and a typical program will keep
one in an automatic variable for the duration of parsing.

\section{The PEGDefinition Container}\label{ch27-the-pegdefinition-container}

\texttt{PEGDefinition} is the grammar container. Its public surface
consists of the \texttt{parent} pointer, the
\texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule} template, the
\texttt{rules(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} accessor, and the
\texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} entry point. The container
itself holds rules by value, compiles each rule's pattern on insertion,
and assigns each rule a monotonically increasing \texttt{order} field
that records declaration precedence.

The \texttt{rules(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} member returns a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}const PEGRule*\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
covering all rules visible at this level. For a root grammar, that is
exactly the locally appended rules in insertion order. For a derived
grammar, parent rules come first, followed by local rules, preserving
the PEG ordered-choice precedence that parent behaviour is consulted
before child overrides. This flattened view is what
\texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} iterates over.

\begin{code}
std::vector<const PEGRule *> visible = grammar.rules();
\end{code}

The container does not expose mutation of existing rules beyond the
references returned by \texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule}. Because
\texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule} returns a \texttt{PEGRule\&}, callers
may configure the rule after insertion: setting its channel, toggling
\texttt{must\_\allowbreak{}fail}, or attaching an
\texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} follow-up rule. These post-insertion
mutations are how advanced behaviour is expressed, and they are
documented in the second half of this chapter.

\section{Adding Rules with add\_rule}\label{ch27-adding-rules-with-add_rule}

Rules are added through the \texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule} template,
which takes a \texttt{FixedString} non-type template parameter (Chapter
4) for the pattern and a callable for the semantic action. The pattern
is therefore a compile-time string literal, which lets the engine verify
the pattern at compile time and avoid runtime parsing of the pattern
text on every call. The action is wrapped in a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function\textless{}\allowbreak{}void(\allowbreak{}PEGMatch\&)\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
and stored inside the rule.

The two overloads, quoted verbatim from the header, are:

\begin{code}
template <FixedString S, typename Fn>
PEGRule &
add_rule (Fn &&action)
{
  return emplace_rule (S.view (),
                       PEGSemanticAction (std::forward<Fn> (action)));
}

template <FixedString S>
PEGRule &
add_rule ()
{
  return emplace_rule (S.view (), PEGSemanticAction{});
}
\end{code}

The first overload is the common form: a pattern and a callback. The
second overload adds a rule with an empty action, useful when the rule
is intended only to advance the position or when the action will be
attached later through the returned reference. Both overloads delegate
to a private \texttt{emplace\_\allowbreak{}rule} that compiles the
pattern, throws
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}invalid\_\allowbreak{}argument}
on a malformed pattern, and stores the rule.

A typical call site binds the returned reference to a name so that the
rule can be configured afterwards. The pattern string uses the Chapter 9
regex subset: literals, the \texttt{.\allowbreak{}} wildcard, character
classes \texttt{[.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}]} with
ranges and \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} negation, and the quantifiers
\texttt{*}, \texttt{+}, \texttt{?}, and the negative lookahead
\texttt{!}. Anchors \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} and \texttt{\$} are
accepted and silently ignored, since PEG matching is implicitly anchored
at the current position.

\begin{code}
auto &identifier = grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">(
    [](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "id:" << m.value << "\n"; });
\end{code}

Because the pattern is a \texttt{FixedString} NTTP, escape sequences
inside the string literal must be written exactly as they would appear
in an ordinary C++ string. The whitespace pattern
\texttt{"[ \textbackslash{}\textbackslash{}t\textbackslash{}\textbackslash{}n]+"}
is the canonical example: the backslashes are part of the C++ string
literal and produce the characters \texttt{\textbackslash{}t} and
\texttt{\textbackslash{}n} inside the \texttt{FixedString}, which the
Chapter 9 matcher then interprets as tab and newline.

\section{The PEGRule Record}\label{ch27-the-pegrule-record}

A \texttt{PEGRule} is the runtime representation of a single production.
Its public members are the compiled pattern, the semantic action, the
channel, and three advanced fields governing negated and follow-up
matching. The complete layout, quoted from the header, is:

\begin{code}
struct PEGRule
{
  std::string pattern_text{};
  peg_detail::CompiledPattern compiled{};
  PEGSemanticAction semantic_action{};
  PEGChannel channel{ "@DEFAULT" };
  bool must_fail{ false };    ///< succeed only when the pattern fails
  bool fire_on_neg{ false };  ///< run the action even on negated success
  PEGRule *if_succeed{ nullptr }; ///< rule to attempt when pattern matches
  std::size_t order{ 0 };     ///< declaration order (PEG precedence)
  // ...
};
\end{code}

\texttt{pattern\_\allowbreak{}text} retains the original pattern string
for diagnostics. \texttt{compiled} holds the runtime-compiled
\texttt{CompiledPattern} produced by the Chapter 9 compiler; it is the
object actually consulted during matching.
\texttt{semantic\_\allowbreak{}action} is the user-supplied callback.
\texttt{channel} is the routing channel, discussed in the next section.
The remaining fields --- \texttt{must\_\allowbreak{}fail},
\texttt{fire\_\allowbreak{}on\_\allowbreak{}neg},
\texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} --- control advanced behaviour that is
outside the main flow of this chapter but is documented here for
completeness.

The \texttt{order} field is assigned by
\texttt{emplace\_\allowbreak{}rule} from a monotonically increasing
counter on the owning definition. It is preserved for diagnostic use and
to stabilise sorting; \texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} does
not consult \texttt{order} directly because the flattened
\texttt{rules(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} vector already encodes
precedence by position. Callers should not rely on specific numeric
values of \texttt{order}, only on its monotonicity within a definition.

\texttt{PEGRule} is movable but not copyable through the usual vector
semantics; it is stored by value in the definition's internal vector.
References returned by \texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule} remain valid
while the vector's storage is stable, which holds as long as rules are
only appended and the definition is not moved. Programs that store rule
references across moves of the definition should re-obtain them after
the move.

\section{The try\_match Member}\label{ch27-the-try_match-member}

The matching core of a rule is \texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}match}, which
takes the input text and a start offset and returns
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional\textless{}\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}size\_\allowbreak{}t\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}:
the end offset on success, or
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}nullopt} on failure. The
implementation honours \texttt{must\_\allowbreak{}fail}: when a rule is
configured to fail-succeed, \texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}match} returns
success exactly when the underlying pattern does not match, consuming
one character so that the tokenizer always makes progress.

\begin{code}
std::optional<std::size_t>
try_match (std::string_view text, std::size_t pos) const
{
  if (must_fail)
    {
      if (compiled.match_at (text, pos))
        return std::nullopt;
      return pos < text.size () ? std::optional<std::size_t> (pos + 1)
                                : std::nullopt;
    }
  return compiled.match_at (text, pos);
}
\end{code}

For ordinary (non-negated) rules, \texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}match} is a
thin forwarder to
\texttt{CompiledPattern:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}match\_\allowbreak{}at},
which performs a greedy prefix match. Greedy quantifiers (\texttt{*},
\texttt{+}, \texttt{?}) consume as much input as they can; the
\texttt{!} quantifier is a zero-width negative lookahead that succeeds
without consuming when its atom does not match. A pattern fails as soon
as any atom cannot be satisfied at the current position.

Callers normally do not invoke \texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}match}
directly;
\texttt{PEGDefinition:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
and the \texttt{PEGMatcher} of Chapter 28 are the intended entry points.
The member is public so that advanced users can probe a rule against
arbitrary positions, for example when integrating the PEG engine with a
hand-written driver.

\section{Channels: Routing Matches}\label{ch27-channels-routing-matches}

Channels are the PEG engine's mechanism for categorising or suppressing
matches without altering the grammar. A channel is a \texttt{PEGChannel}
value: a thin wrapper around a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string} name with equality
comparison and an
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}ignore(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} predicate.
The header predeclares two built-in channels and provides a factory for
custom ones.

\begin{code}
struct PEGChannel
{
  std::string name{ "@DEFAULT" };
  constexpr bool operator== (const PEGChannel &) const = default;
  bool is_ignore () const noexcept { return name == "@IGNORE"; }
};

inline const PEGChannel PEGIgnoreChannel{ "@IGNORE" };
inline const PEGChannel PEGDefaultChannel{ "@DEFAULT" };
\end{code}

\texttt{PEGDefaultChanne\allowbreak{}l} (named \texttt{@DEFAULT}) is the
channel assigned to every newly created rule. Matches on the default
channel are ordinary semantic tokens: they are reported through the
\texttt{PEGMatch} passed to the action, and they are visible to the
matcher arms described in Chapter 28.
\texttt{PEGIgnoreChannel\allowbreak{}} (named \texttt{@IGNORE}) marks a
rule as ignorable. The
\texttt{PEGMatch:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ignored} flag is set to
\texttt{true} for such matches, and the matcher of Chapter 28 skips them
silently before consulting any arm.

Assigning a rule to the ignore channel is a one-line mutation of the
returned reference. This is the canonical way to handle whitespace and
comments: declare a rule whose pattern matches the ignorable text, give
it an action that does nothing (or does bookkeeping only), and route it
to \texttt{@IGNORE}. The grammar will still consume the text and advance
the position, but downstream consumers will not see it as a token.

\begin{code}
auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \\t\\n]+">(
    [](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { /* no-op */ });
ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;
\end{code}

The decision to route a match to a channel is a property of the rule,
not of the match. Two matches produced by the same rule always share the
rule's channel; there is no per-match override. This keeps the model
simple and predictable: the channel is part of the rule's identity, like
its pattern and its action.

\section{Custom Channels}\label{ch27-custom-channels}

Beyond the two built-ins, the engine supports user-defined channels
through \texttt{new\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}channel}. A custom
channel is just a \texttt{PEGChannel} with a caller-chosen name, subject
to the single constraint that the name begins with \texttt{@}. The
factory enforces this at construction time and throws
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}invalid\_\allowbreak{}argument}
otherwise.

\begin{code}
inline PEGChannel
new_peg_channel (std::string_view name)
{
  if (name.empty () || name.front () != '@')
    throw std::invalid_argument (
        "dsl::new_peg_channel: channel names must begin with '@'");
  return PEGChannel{ std::string (name) };
}
\end{code}

Custom channels are useful when a grammar produces several categories of
token that consumers wish to filter independently. A compiler front end,
for example, might route comments to \texttt{@COMMENTS}, string literals
to \texttt{@STRINGS}, and ordinary tokens to \texttt{@DEFAULT}. The
matcher of Chapter 28 exposes \texttt{only}, \texttt{include}, and
\texttt{exclude} filters that select arms by channel, so categorising at
the rule level pays off when consuming the stream.

\begin{code}
const dsl::PEGChannel CommentsChannel = dsl::new_peg_channel ("@COMMENTS");

auto &line_comment = grammar.add_rule<"//[^\\n]*">(
    [](dsl::PEGMatch &) { /* swallow */ });
line_comment.channel = CommentsChannel;
\end{code}

Note that custom channels are not ignorable by default; only
\texttt{@IGNORE} triggers the
\texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}ignore(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} predicate.
A custom channel is visible to consumers unless the consumer explicitly
filters it out. Routing whitespace to a custom non-ignore channel would
cause it to be reported as an ordinary token, which is rarely what is
wanted; for whitespace, use \texttt{PEGIgnoreChannel\allowbreak{}}.

\section{The PEGMatch Record}\label{ch27-the-pegmatch-record}

When a rule matches, the engine constructs a \texttt{PEGMatch}
describing the match and passes it by reference to the rule's semantic
action. The record carries the matched text as a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view}, the
begin and end offsets in the source, a pointer to the producing rule,
the resolved channel, optional user data, and 1-based line and column
information.

\begin{code}
struct PEGMatch
{
  std::string_view value{};   ///< matched text
  std::size_t begin{ 0 };     ///< start offset in source
  std::size_t end{ 0 };       ///< one-past-end offset in source
  PEGRule *rule{ nullptr };   ///< rule that produced this match
  PEGChannel channel{};       ///< resolved channel of the rule
  void *user_data{ nullptr }; ///< optional extension point
  std::size_t line{ 1 };      ///< 1-based line of `begin`
  std::size_t column{ 1 };    ///< 1-based column of `begin`
  bool ignored{ false };      ///< true if routed to @IGNORE
  std::size_t length () const noexcept { return end - begin; }
};
\end{code}

The \texttt{value} view is a slice into the original input text passed
to \texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. It is valid only for the
duration of the parse; actions that need to retain the matched text must
copy it into an owning \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}.
The \texttt{begin} and \texttt{end} offsets are absolute positions in
the source, and \texttt{length(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is provided
as a convenience for the common \texttt{end - begin} query.

The \texttt{rule} pointer lets an action inspect the rule that produced
the match, including its pattern text and channel. This is occasionally
useful when a single action lambda is shared across several rules via
capture: the action can branch on
\texttt{m.\allowbreak{}rule-\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}pattern\_\allowbreak{}text}
or on \texttt{m.\allowbreak{}rule-\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}order}. The
\texttt{user\_\allowbreak{}data} field is an opaque extension point left
at \texttt{nullptr} by the engine; callers may stash a pointer to an
external state object there through the rule reference before parsing.

Line and column are computed eagerly by the engine using a helper that
walks the input once per match. They are 1-based, with columns counting
characters from the start of the line. This makes diagnostic messages
from semantic actions directly usable without further computation.

\section{Semantic Actions}\label{ch27-semantic-actions}

A semantic action is any callable convertible to
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function\textless{}\allowbreak{}void(\allowbreak{}PEGMatch\&)\allowbreak{}\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
The action is invoked after a rule matches but before the position is
advanced, so it may inspect the surrounding input through the original
\texttt{text} if it has access to it (typically via capture). The
action's return value, if any, is discarded. Actions are stored inside
the rule and persist for the lifetime of the definition.

The signature \texttt{void(\allowbreak{}PEGMatch\&)\allowbreak{}} is the
contract. An action may record a token into a vector captured by
reference, build an AST node using the facilities of Chapter 10 and
Chapter 11, emit diagnostics, or do nothing at all. The engine does not
interpret the action's effects; it merely calls it. This makes the PEG
engine agnostic to the downstream representation, in contrast to the
parser combinators of Chapter 23 which thread a parser state through the
call.

\begin{code}
std::vector<std::string> tokens;
auto &identifier = grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">(
    [&tokens](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { tokens.emplace_back (m.value); });
\end{code}

Actions on ignore-channel rules are still invoked. The
\texttt{m.\allowbreak{}ignored} flag is set to \texttt{true}, which lets
a shared action distinguish ignorable matches from ordinary ones. A
typical whitespace action does nothing, but it could equally well count
lines or record comment text for a documentation tool.

Because actions are \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function}
values, they incur the usual overhead of type erasure and dynamic
dispatch. For the grammars the PEG engine is designed for --- tokenisers
and small scanners --- this cost is negligible relative to the cost of
pattern matching itself. Callers concerned with overhead may pass
stateless lambdas, which are stored efficiently inside
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}function}.

\section{The parse Entry Point}\label{ch27-the-parse-entry-point}

\texttt{PEGDefinition:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse(\allowbreak{}std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string\_\allowbreak{}view text)\allowbreak{}}
is the entry point that runs the rule set over an input. It returns a
\texttt{PEGParseResult} summarising the outcome: whether the whole input
was consumed, the final offset, and on failure a human-readable message
plus the line and column of the failing position.

\begin{code}
PEGParseResult
parse (std::string_view text) const
{
  PEGParseResult result;
  auto all = rules ();
  std::size_t pos = 0;
  while (pos < text.size ())
    {
      bool matched = false;
      for (const PEGRule *r : all)
        {
          auto end = r->try_match (text, pos);
          if (!end)
            continue;
          // ... fire action, advance pos, break ...
        }
      // ...
    }
  result.offset = pos;
  return result;
}
\end{code}

The algorithm is a straightforward ordered-choice loop. At each
position, the engine iterates over the flattened rule list and calls
\texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}match} on each rule. The first rule that
returns a non-empty match wins; its action is invoked and the position
is advanced to the match end. If no rule matches, the parse fails at the
current offset. If a rule matches empty input (a zero-width positive
match), the parse fails immediately to avoid an infinite loop.

The \texttt{parse} member is \texttt{const}, so it may be called on a
temporary grammar or on a grammar held by \texttt{const} reference. It
does not mutate the definition or its rules; semantic actions that need
to accumulate state must capture external storage by reference. The
constness also means that channels configured before the call are
honoured as-is, and actions installed before the call are the ones that
fire.

\section{Ordered Choice in Detail}\label{ch27-ordered-choice-in-detail}

Ordered choice is the defining semantics of PEG, and the \texttt{parse}
loop implements it faithfully. The flattened rule list returned by
\texttt{rules(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is consulted in order: parent
rules first, then local rules in declaration order. The first rule whose
\texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}match} returns a non-empty result is the
winner; later rules at the same position are not tried. This is the
meaning of the \texttt{break} statement that closes the inner loop.

Because choice is ordered, the sequence in which rules are added
matters. A more specific rule must precede a more general one, or it
will never be reached. The classic case is keywords versus identifiers:
a rule for the literal \texttt{if} must be declared before the rule for
\texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}][a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}0-9]*}, otherwise
the identifier rule will consume \texttt{if} as an identifier and the
keyword rule will never see it.

\begin{code}
auto &kw_if    = grammar.add_rule<"if">   (print_kw);
auto &kw_then  = grammar.add_rule<"then"> (print_kw);
auto &kw_else  = grammar.add_rule<"else"> (print_kw);
auto &ident    = grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*"> (print_id);
\end{code}

Ordered choice also means that a broad pattern early in the list can
shadow a narrower one later. The engine does not perform longest-match
analysis across rules; the first match wins regardless of length. A rule
matching \texttt{[a-z]+} placed before a rule matching \texttt{if} will
always consume the \texttt{if} prefix, leaving the second rule
unreachable. Authors must therefore order rules from most specific to
least specific.

This differs from regular-expression alternation, where the longest
match typically wins across alternatives. The PEG engine's rule is
simpler and cheaper: first match wins. Authors accustomed to regex
alternation should be careful to translate alternatives into ordered
rules, not into a single pattern with \texttt{|} (which the Chapter 9
subset does not support).

\section{The PEGParseResult}\label{ch27-the-pegparseresult}

The result of \texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} is a
\texttt{PEGParseResult}, a small aggregate with success/failure flags,
position information, and a message string. Its public interface, quoted
from the header, is:

\begin{code}
struct PEGParseResult
{
  bool success{ true };
  std::size_t offset{ 0 };
  std::size_t line{ 1 };
  std::size_t column{ 1 };
  std::string message{};

  bool ok () const noexcept { return success; }
  bool failed () const noexcept { return !success; }
  std::size_t error_offset () const noexcept { return offset; }
  std::size_t error_line () const noexcept { return line; }
  std::size_t error_column () const noexcept { return column; }
  std::string_view error_message () const noexcept { return message; }
};
\end{code}

On success, \texttt{offset} holds the final position (equal to
\texttt{text.\allowbreak{}size(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} when the
whole input was consumed), \texttt{success} is \texttt{true}, and
\texttt{message} is empty. On failure, \texttt{success} is
\texttt{false}, \texttt{offset}/\texttt{line}/\texttt{column} describe
the position at which parsing stuck, and \texttt{message} is a
human-readable diagnostic such as
\texttt{"no PEG rule mat\allowbreak{}ched at offset 1\allowbreak{}7"}.
Callers typically branch on \texttt{ok(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} or
\texttt{failed(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and then format the
diagnostic for the user.

The two failure modes are distinguishable by message. A failure with the
message
\texttt{"no PEG rule mat\allowbreak{}ched at offset N\allowbreak{}"}
indicates that no visible rule could match at the current position ---
typically a syntax error or a missing rule. A failure with the message
\texttt{"PEG rule matche\allowbreak{}d empty input; r\allowbreak{}efusing to loop"\allowbreak{}}
indicates that a rule matched zero characters, which the engine refuses
to accept because it would not advance the position. The latter almost
always indicates a pattern bug, such as \texttt{[x]*} used without a
following required atom.

\section{A Whitespace-Ignoring
Tokenizer}\label{ch27-a-whitespace-ignoring-tokenizer}

The first complete example mirrors
\texttt{examples/\allowbreak{}23-peg-definitio\allowbreak{}n.\allowbreak{}cpp}.
It defines a grammar that skips whitespace, recognises a handful of
keywords, identifiers, numbers, and operators, and parses a short input.
Each rule prints its match; the whitespace rule is routed to the ignore
channel.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int
main ()
{
  auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();

  auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \\t\\n]+"> (
      [](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "skip_ws:" << m.length () << "\n"; });
  ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;

  auto &kw_if   = grammar.add_rule<"if">   ([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "kw:"   << m.value << "\n"; });
  auto &kw_then = grammar.add_rule<"then"> ([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "kw:"   << m.value << "\n"; });
  auto &ident   = grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">(
      [](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "id:"  << m.value << "\n"; });
  auto &number  = grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]+">(
      [](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "num:" << m.value << "\n"; });
  auto &plus    = grammar.add_rule<"+"> ([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "op:" << m.value << "\n"; });
  auto &minus   = grammar.add_rule<"-"> ([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "op:" << m.value << "\n"; });

  auto result = grammar.parse ("if x1 + 2 then 40");
  std::cout << (result.ok () ? "parse_ok" : "parse_failed") << "\n";
  std::cout << "offset=" << result.offset << "\n";
}
\end{code}

The ordering of the \texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule} calls is
significant. The keyword rules precede the identifier rule, so
\texttt{if} and \texttt{then} are recognised as keywords rather than as
identifiers. The whitespace rule is declared first but, because it is
routed to \texttt{@IGNORE}, its position in the list only affects when
it is tried relative to other rules; placing it first means whitespace
is consumed before any token is attempted, which is the usual
convention.

Running this grammar on \texttt{"if x1 + 2 then \allowbreak{}40"}
produces a sequence of \texttt{kw}, \texttt{id}, \texttt{op},
\texttt{num}, \texttt{kw}, and \texttt{num} lines, followed by
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}ok}. The whitespace rule fires between
tokens and prints \texttt{skip\_\allowbreak{}ws} lines, but because it
is on the ignore channel, a downstream consumer using the Chapter 28
matcher would not see those matches as tokens.

\section{A Keyword Grammar}\label{ch27-a-keyword-grammar}

Keywords are the simplest case of an ordering-sensitive grammar. Each
keyword is a literal pattern, and the identifier rule is a broader class
pattern. Declaring keywords first ensures they win over the identifier
rule at positions where the input begins with a keyword.

\begin{code}
auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();
grammar.add_rule<"if">   ([](dsl::PEGMatch &) { /* emit IF    */ });
grammar.add_rule<"then"> ([](dsl::PEGMatch &) { /* emit THEN  */ });
grammar.add_rule<"else"> ([](dsl::PEGMatch &) { /* emit ELSE  */ });
grammar.add_rule<"end">  ([](dsl::PEGMatch &) { /* emit END   */ });
grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">(
    [](dsl::PEGMatch &) { /* emit IDENT */ });
\end{code}

The literal patterns \texttt{"if"}, \texttt{"then"}, \texttt{"else"},
and \texttt{"end"} match exactly those spellings. The identifier pattern
\texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}][a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}0-9]*} would also
match them, but because the keyword rules come first, the keyword rules
win. Swapping the order would make the identifier rule consume every
keyword, and the keyword rules would become unreachable dead code.

A subtle point: the literal pattern \texttt{"if"} matches the prefix
\texttt{if} of the input \texttt{iffy}. Under PEG ordered choice, the
keyword rule wins at the position of the \texttt{i}, consumes two
characters, and leaves \texttt{fy} for the next iteration, where the
identifier rule will match \texttt{fy}. This is rarely what is wanted
for a real language, where keywords should be whole words. The Chapter 9
pattern subset has no word-boundary anchor, so the usual fix is a
negative-lookahead atom: the pattern \texttt{"if!"} would match
\texttt{if} only when not followed by an identifier character. Authors
should test such patterns against inputs like \texttt{iffy} to confirm
the intended behaviour.

\section{Identifiers and Numbers}\label{ch27-identifiers-and-numbers}

Identifiers and numbers are the workhorse patterns of most tokenizers.
The identifier pattern
\texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}][a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}0-9]*} matches a
letter or underscore followed by zero or more letters, digits, or
underscores. The number pattern \texttt{[0-9]+} matches one or more
digits. Both are greedy: they consume as much matching input as
possible.

\begin{code}
auto &ident  = grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">(
    [](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "id:"  << m.value << "\n"; });
auto &number = grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]+">(
    [](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "num:" << m.value << "\n"; });
\end{code}

Greedy matching means \texttt{[0-9]+} consumes the entire digit run of
\texttt{12345} as a single match; it does not stop after \texttt{1} or
\texttt{12}. This is the desired behaviour for a number token. For
identifiers, the greedy run stops at the first character that is not a
letter, digit, or underscore, which correctly terminates \texttt{x1} at
the space.

Numbers with fractional parts, signs, or exponents require more
elaborate patterns. Because the Chapter 9 subset lacks grouping, a
floating-point pattern must be written as a flat sequence:
\texttt{[0-9]+.\allowbreak{}[0-9]+} matches a decimal number with a
fractional part, and \texttt{[0-9]+.\allowbreak{}![0-9]} would match an
integer not followed by a decimal point and digit. Authors should
compose such patterns carefully and test them, as the absence of
grouping makes some common forms awkward to express.

\section{A Small Expression Grammar}\label{ch27-a-small-expression-grammar}

Combining the pieces above yields a small expression tokenizer. The
following grammar recognises identifiers, integer literals, the four
arithmetic operators, and parentheses, ignoring whitespace between
tokens. It does not build a tree; it simply prints each token. Tree
construction is the job of the semantic action, which may use
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf} and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node} from Chapter 11 to assemble
an AST.

\begin{code}
auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();

auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \\t\\n]+">([](dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;

grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "id:"  << m.value << "\n"; });
grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]+">                ([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "num:" << m.value << "\n"; });
grammar.add_rule<"+">                     ([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "op:"  << m.value << "\n"; });
grammar.add_rule<"-">                     ([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "op:"  << m.value << "\n"; });
grammar.add_rule<"*">                     ([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "op:"  << m.value << "\n"; });
grammar.add_rule<"/">                     ([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "op:"  << m.value << "\n"; });
grammar.add_rule<"(">([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "lp:"   << m.value << "\n"; });
grammar.add_rule<")">([](dsl::PEGMatch &m) { std::cout << "rp:"   << m.value << "\n"; });

auto r = grammar.parse ("(a + 42) * (b - 7)");
std::cout << (r.ok () ? "ok" : "fail:" + std::string (r.error_message ())) << "\n";
\end{code}

Note that the operator rules are single-character literals. The Chapter
9 matcher treats \texttt{+}, \texttt{-}, \texttt{*},
\texttt{/\allowbreak{}}, \texttt{(\allowbreak{}}, and
\texttt{)\allowbreak{}} as literal atoms, so each rule matches exactly
that character. There is no ambiguity with quantifiers here, because the
quantifier characters only have their special meaning when they follow
an atom; as the sole content of a pattern, \texttt{+} is a literal plus
sign.

A real expression parser would not stop at tokenisation. The semantic
actions would push tokens onto a stack, and a higher-level driver would
reduce the stack into an AST using precedence rules. That reduction is
outside the scope of the PEG engine itself, which is intentionally a
scanner; the recursive-structure parsing is the domain of the
combinators in Chapters 23 through 25 or of hand-written logic over the
token stream.

\section{Building a Token Vector}\label{ch27-building-a-token-vector}

Printing tokens to \texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}cout} is
illustrative but not reusable. A more practical action captures a vector
by reference and appends a structured token to it. The token type is
arbitrary; the example below uses a simple struct with a kind tag and
the matched text.

\begin{code}
struct Token { std::string kind; std::string text; std::size_t line; };

std::vector<Token> tokens;
auto emit = [&](std::string kind) {
    return [&tokens, kind = std::move (kind)](dsl::PEGMatch &m) {
        tokens.push_back (Token{ kind, std::string (m.value), m.line });
    };
};

auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();
grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*"> (emit ("ident"));
grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]+">                 (emit ("number"));
grammar.add_rule<"+">                      (emit ("plus"));
grammar.add_rule<"-">                      (emit ("minus"));

auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \\t]+">([](dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;

grammar.parse ("alpha + 3 - beta");
// tokens now holds: ident(alpha), plus(+), number(3), minus(-), ident(beta)
\end{code}

The \texttt{emit} helper returns a lambda bound to a specific kind
string. Each rule's action is a distinct closure sharing the
\texttt{tokens} vector by reference. Because the action is invoked
synchronously during \texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}, the
vector is fully populated by the time
\texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} returns, and the order of
tokens matches the order of matches in the input.

This pattern scales to grammars of moderate size. For larger grammars,
the kind tag is often an enumeration rather than a string, and the token
struct carries additional fields such as the begin offset and the
channel. The \texttt{PEGMatch} record provides all of these, so the
action can copy whichever fields are relevant.

\section{Building an AST with
Actions}\label{ch27-building-an-ast-with-actions}

The semantic action is the natural place to construct AST nodes. The
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}ASTNode} value type from Chapter
10 and the \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}leaf} and
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node} builders from Chapter 11
are available for this purpose. An identifier action might push a leaf
node onto a stack; an operator action might pop two operands and push a
binary node.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <vector>
#include <string>

std::vector<dsl::ASTNode> stack;

auto push_leaf = [](std::string tag) {
    return [&stack, tag = std::move (tag)](dsl::PEGMatch &m) {
        stack.push_back (dsl::leaf (tag, std::string (m.value)));
    };
};

auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();
grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*"> (push_leaf ("ident"));
grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]+">                 (push_leaf ("number"));
\end{code}

The operator actions would then reduce the stack: pop two children,
combine them with
\texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}node (\allowbreak{}"add",\allowbreak{} \{ lhs,\allowbreak{} rhs \})\allowbreak{}},
and push the result. Because the PEG engine is a scanner and not a
precedence-aware parser, the reduction logic must live in the action or
in a driver that consumes the token stream. The combinators of Chapter
23 offer an alternative model in which precedence is expressed
structurally rather than by post-hoc reduction.

The choice between the PEG scanner plus hand-written reduction, and the
parser combinators of Chapter 23, is a design decision. The PEG engine
is lighter and faster for tokenising; the combinators are more
expressive for full grammar parsing. The two are designed to coexist,
and the PEG engine's tokens can feed a combinator-based parser where
appropriate.

\section{Derived Grammars}\label{ch27-derived-grammars}

A derived grammar extends a parent grammar without mutating it. The
factory
\texttt{derive\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}definition(\allowbreak{}parent)\allowbreak{}}
returns a new \texttt{PEGDefinition} whose \texttt{parent} pointer
references the parent. The derived grammar sees all parent rules first
in the flattened rule list, followed by its own local rules, so parent
behaviour is consulted before child overrides at every position.

\begin{code}
auto base = dsl::create_peg_definition ();
base.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_]+">([](dsl::PEGMatch &) { /* ident */ });
base.add_rule<"[0-9]+">    ([](dsl::PEGMatch &) { /* number */ });

auto extended = dsl::derive_peg_definition (base);
extended.add_rule<"0x[0-9a-fA-F]+">([](dsl::PEGMatch &) { /* hex */ });

auto r = extended.parse ("0x1F");
\end{code}

Derived grammars are the PEG engine's answer to grammar inheritance. A
base grammar might define the common lexical structure of a family of
languages, and each derived grammar might add language-specific tokens.
Because the parent is consulted first, a derived grammar can also
effectively shadow a parent rule by adding a more specific rule earlier
in its own local list --- though true shadowing of a parent rule at the
same position is not possible, since the parent rule wins by precedence.

The parent pointer is a raw \texttt{const PEGDefinit\allowbreak{}ion*}.
The caller is responsible for ensuring the parent outlives the derived
grammar. Deriving from a temporary parent is a common error: the derived
grammar holds a dangling pointer, and
\texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} will exhibit undefined
behaviour. The usual discipline is to keep both grammars in named
variables with overlapping lifetimes.

\section{Rule Configuration After
Insertion}\label{ch27-rule-configuration-after-insertion}

The reference returned by \texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule} is the primary
handle for post-insertion configuration. Beyond setting the channel,
callers may toggle \texttt{must\_\allowbreak{}fail}, set
\texttt{fire\_\allowbreak{}on\_\allowbreak{}neg}, or attach an
\texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} follow-up rule. These advanced fields
are summarised here for completeness; their use is uncommon but
supported.

\texttt{must\_\allowbreak{}fail} inverts a rule's success condition.
When \texttt{true}, the rule succeeds only when its pattern does not
match at the current position, and it consumes one character on success.
This is the engine's mechanism for negative lookahead at the rule level,
distinct from the \texttt{!} quantifier which operates within a single
pattern. A \texttt{must\_\allowbreak{}fail} rule is useful for
implementing ``any character except those starting X'' without
enumerating the excluded set.

\texttt{fire\_\allowbreak{}on\_\allowbreak{}neg} controls whether a
\texttt{must\_\allowbreak{}fail} rule's action is invoked. By default, a
negated rule fires no action, since there is no matched text to report.
Setting \texttt{fire\_\allowbreak{}on\_\allowbreak{}neg = true} causes
the action to run with a \texttt{PEGMatch} describing the single
consumed character, which is occasionally useful for diagnostic logging.

\texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} is a pointer to a second rule that is
tried at the same position after the primary rule matches. If the
follow-up rule also matches, its action is invoked with its own
\texttt{PEGMatch}. The follow-up does not advance the position further;
the position advances to the end of the primary match. This mechanism
supports limited forms of lookahead-with-action within the definition
model, though most grammars will not need it.

\begin{code}
auto &kw_if  = grammar.add_rule<"if"> ([](dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
auto &not_id = grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_0-9]">([](dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
not_id.must_fail = true;
kw_if.if_succeed = &not_id; // match "if" only when not followed by an ident char
\end{code}

These advanced features are entirely optional. A grammar that uses only
patterns, actions, and channels will never touch
\texttt{must\_\allowbreak{}fail},
\texttt{fire\_\allowbreak{}on\_\allowbreak{}neg}, or
\texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed}, and that is the expected case for the
majority of users.

\section{Patterns and the Chapter 9
Subset}\label{ch27-patterns-and-the-chapter-9-subset}

Every rule pattern is compiled by the same
\texttt{CompiledPattern:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}compile} function
used by the \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}pattern} engine of
Chapter 9. The accepted syntax is therefore identical: literal
characters, the \texttt{.\allowbreak{}} wildcard, character classes
\texttt{[.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}]} with ranges
(\texttt{a-z}) and \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} negation, and the
quantifiers \texttt{*} (zero or more), \texttt{+} (one or more),
\texttt{?} (zero or one), and \texttt{!} (zero-width negative
lookahead). The anchors \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} and \texttt{\$} are
accepted and ignored.

What the subset does not provide is just as important. There is no
alternation operator \texttt{|}: alternatives are expressed as separate
rules, not within a single pattern. There is no parenthesised grouping:
a quantifier applies only to the immediately preceding atom, so
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}ab)\allowbreak{}+} is not expressible. There are
no backreferences, no non-greedy quantifiers, and no named captures.
Authors translating a regex to a PEG rule pattern should decompose
alternatives into ordered rules and flatten any grouped quantifiers.

The \texttt{!} quantifier is the subset's negative lookahead. It does
not consume input; it succeeds at the current position when its atom
does not match. The pattern \texttt{"if!"} is therefore \texttt{if}
followed by a negative lookahead on\ldots{} nothing, because \texttt{!}
must follow an atom. To express ``if not followed by an identifier
character'', the correct pattern is
\texttt{"if![a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}0-9]"}, where the \texttt{!} applies
to the class \texttt{[a-zA-Z\_\allowbreak{}0-9]}. Authors should verify
their understanding of \texttt{!} against small examples, as its
zero-width semantics can be surprising.

Malformed patterns are rejected at \texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule} time.
The compiler sets a \texttt{valid} flag and an \texttt{error} string on
the \texttt{CompiledPattern}, and \texttt{emplace\_\allowbreak{}rule}
throws
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}invalid\_\allowbreak{}argument}
with a message combining the error and the offending pattern. The only
currently detected malformation is an unterminated character class (a
missing \texttt{]}); other syntactic issues are not diagnosed at compile
time and will simply produce a pattern that matches in unexpected ways.

\section{Anchors and Implicit
Anchoring}\label{ch27-anchors-and-implicit-anchoring}

PEG matching is implicitly anchored at the current position. There is no
notion of searching for a pattern anywhere in the remaining input; a
rule either matches starting exactly at \texttt{pos} or it fails. The
\texttt{\textasciicircum{}} and \texttt{\$} anchors of regex syntax are
therefore redundant, and the compiler accepts and ignores them to ease
porting of existing patterns.

This implicit anchoring is why the engine is a scanner rather than a
searcher. Each iteration of the \texttt{parse} loop fixes a position,
and the rules compete to match at that position. A rule whose pattern
begins with \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} behaves identically to one
without it; a rule ending with \texttt{\$} matches only when the end of
the pattern coincides with the end of input, which is already enforced
by the greedy match reaching the end of the available text.

Authors sometimes attempt to use \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} to mean
``match only at the start of the input''. The engine does not honour
that meaning; \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} is ignored. A rule that should
match only at the start of input must be handled by driver logic outside
the grammar, for example by parsing the first token separately and then
parsing the remainder with the full grammar.

\section{Greedy Matching and
Backtracking}\label{ch27-greedy-matching-and-backtracking}

The Chapter 9 matcher is greedy: each quantifier consumes as much
matching input as possible. There is no backtracking across atoms within
a pattern, and no backtracking across rules. Once a rule's
\texttt{try\_\allowbreak{}match} returns an end offset, that offset is
the position the engine advances to; later rules are not tried at the
original position, and the matching rule is not retried with a shorter
match.

This makes the engine's behaviour easy to predict. A pattern
\texttt{[a-z]+} followed by \texttt{[0-9]} will fail on input
\texttt{abc} because the greedy \texttt{[a-z]+} consumes all three
letters and leaves nothing for the required digit. There is no mechanism
for \texttt{[a-z]+} to give back a character so that \texttt{[0-9]} can
match. Authors must order atoms so that greedy quantifiers do not starve
later atoms; the negative lookahead \texttt{!} is the usual tool for
bounding a greedy run.

The absence of cross-rule backtracking is the practical consequence of
ordered choice. Once a rule wins at a position, the engine commits to
it. If the rule's match leads to a dead end later in the input, the
engine does not revisit the position to try an alternative rule. This is
faithful to PEG semantics and is one of the reasons PEG parsing is
efficient: there is no speculative exploration.

\section{The if\_succeed Hook}\label{ch27-the-if_succeed-hook}

The \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} hook is the engine's constrained
form of lookahead-with-action. When a rule with an
\texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} pointer matches, the engine
immediately tries the pointed-to rule at the same position. If that rule
also matches, its action runs with a \texttt{PEGMatch} covering the
second rule's span. The position then advances to the end of the primary
match, not the second match.

This is useful when a primary token should be recognised only when
followed by a specific context, but the context should not be consumed
as part of the token. For example, a rule for the keyword \texttt{if}
might attach an \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} rule that looks for
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}}, so that the action can record that the
\texttt{if} begins a parenthesised condition. The
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}} itself is then matched again by the normal
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}} rule on the next iteration.

The \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} hook is a pointer to another rule
in the same definition. The caller is responsible for ensuring the
pointed-to rule outlives the referencing rule, which is automatic when
both are stored in the same \texttt{PEGDefinition}. Pointing across
definitions, especially from a derived grammar to a parent rule, is
possible but fragile: the parent must outlive the derived grammar, and
the pointed-to rule must remain in effect.

Most grammars will not need \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed}. It is
documented here so that readers encountering it in existing code
understand its semantics, and so that authors facing a genuine
lookahead-with-action requirement know it exists. For ordinary lookahead
without an action, the \texttt{!} quantifier within a single pattern is
simpler and sufficient.

\section{Pitfalls in Rule Ordering}\label{ch27-pitfalls-in-rule-ordering}

Rule ordering is the most common source of surprising behaviour in PEG
grammars. The engine's first-match-wins rule means that a broad rule
placed early will shadow every narrower rule placed after it. The
identifier-versus-keyword case is the canonical example, but the same
issue arises whenever one pattern is a prefix language of another.

A less obvious case arises with operators. A rule for
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}} followed by a rule for
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=} will never recognise
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=}, because the
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}} rule matches first and consumes only
the first character. The fix is to declare the longer operator first:
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}=} before
\texttt{\textless{}\allowbreak{}}, \texttt{==} before \texttt{=},
\texttt{-\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} before \texttt{-}. This
longest-first ordering is a general principle for literal-prefix
ambiguities.

Whitespace rules interact with ordering too. A whitespace rule placed
after a token rule will not be tried at a position where the token rule
matches, so whitespace between tokens is only consumed because the token
rule fails on the whitespace character. Placing the whitespace rule
first is the usual convention and ensures whitespace is skipped before
any token is attempted. Routing it to \texttt{@IGNORE} then makes it
invisible to consumers.

A subtle pitfall is the zero-width match. A rule whose pattern can match
the empty string --- for example \texttt{[a-z]*} at a position where the
next character is a digit --- will cause
\texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} to fail with the message
\texttt{"PEG rule matche\allowbreak{}d empty input; r\allowbreak{}efusing to loop"\allowbreak{}}.
The engine refuses to advance on a zero-width match, because doing so
would loop forever. Patterns that can match empty should be rewritten to
require at least one character (\texttt{[a-z]+}) or guarded by a
following required atom.

\section{Pitfalls with Ignore-Channel
Rules}\label{ch27-pitfalls-with-ignore-channel-rules}

Routing a rule to \texttt{@IGNORE} does not remove it from the
ordered-choice loop; it only changes how its matches are reported. An
ignore-channel rule must still match in order to advance the position.
If the ignore rule is the only rule that can match at a given position
and it fails, the parse fails, just as it would for a default-channel
rule.

This means that an ignore-channel whitespace rule must cover every form
of whitespace the input may contain. A rule for
\texttt{[ \textbackslash{}t]+} will not consume newlines, so an input
containing a newline at a position where no other rule matches will
cause the parse to fail. The broader pattern
\texttt{[ \textbackslash{}t\textbackslash{}n\textbackslash{}r]+} is
safer and is the usual choice for cross-platform whitespace handling.

Ignore-channel rules still fire their actions. The
\texttt{m.\allowbreak{}ignored} flag is set to \texttt{true}, but the
action runs as normal. An action that does heavy work on every match
will do that work for ignorable matches too; if that is not desired, the
action should check \texttt{m.\allowbreak{}ignored} and return early. A
no-op action is the simplest choice for whitespace and comments.

A final pitfall concerns channel assignment timing. The channel is read
from the rule at match time, not at insertion time. Changing a rule's
channel after some matches have already occurred affects subsequent
matches but not past ones. In practice this is rarely an issue because
channels are configured before parsing begins, but it is worth noting
that the channel is a mutable property of the rule.

\section{Derivation and Shadowing}\label{ch27-derivation-and-shadowing}

Derived grammars inherit parent rules, but they cannot truly shadow a
parent rule at the same position. Because parent rules come first in the
flattened list, a parent rule that matches at a position will always win
over a child rule that could also match there. A child grammar can only
add new rules for cases the parent does not cover, or override parent
behaviour by mutation of the parent rule (which requires non-const
access to the parent).

This is a deliberate design choice. It makes derivation additive and
non-destructive: a parent grammar's behaviour is preserved exactly when
viewed through a derived grammar. Callers who need true shadowing should
construct a fresh grammar rather than deriving, or should restructure
the parent so that the shadowed rule is less specific and can be
overridden by a more specific child rule placed earlier.

The \texttt{rules(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} accessor flattens parent
and child rules into a single vector, which
\texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} iterates over. There is no
per-iteration lookup of parent versus child; the flattened list is the
complete rule set as far as the engine is concerned. This keeps the
parse loop simple and avoids virtual dispatch or rule-lookup overhead.

When a derived grammar is itself used as a parent for further
derivation, the flattening recurses through the chain. A grandchild
grammar sees its parent's rules (which include the grandparent's rules)
first, then its own. The depth of the chain is not bounded by the
engine, but very deep chains will produce large flattened lists on every
\texttt{rules(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} call, which is something to
keep in mind for performance-sensitive uses.

\section{Lifetime and Reference
Stability}\label{ch27-lifetime-and-reference-stability}

The \texttt{PEGRule} references returned by
\texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule} are stable as long as the definition's
internal vector does not reallocate. Because rules are only appended,
the vector may reallocate as it grows, but the engine uses a
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}vector\textless{}\allowbreak{}PEGRule\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}
whose references are invalidated by reallocation. In practice, callers
should obtain references after all rules have been added, or should
reserve capacity in advance if the engine exposed such a hook.

The engine does not currently expose a \texttt{reserve} method. Callers
who need absolute reference stability across many insertions may wish to
add rules in batches, or to store rules by pointer in their own
structures. For the typical grammar of a few dozen rules, reallocation
is unlikely to be a problem, but it is a theoretical concern worth
noting.

The \texttt{PEGDefinition} itself is movable. Moving a definition
transfers ownership of its rules to the target, and references obtained
before the move are invalidated. The \texttt{parent} pointer of a
derived grammar is a raw pointer to the parent object; if the parent is
moved, the pointer still refers to the original location, which is now
empty. Callers should avoid moving a grammar that is referenced as a
parent by a derived grammar.

The \texttt{if\_\allowbreak{}succeed} pointer is a raw
\texttt{PEGRule*}. It must point to a rule that outlives the referencing
rule. Within a single definition, this is automatic. Across definitions,
the caller must ensure the pointed-to rule's definition outlives the
referencing rule's definition. The engine does not track these
dependencies; misuse leads to dangling pointers and undefined behaviour.

\section{Integration with the
Matcher}\label{ch27-integration-with-the-matcher}

The \texttt{PEGDefinition} is the input to the \texttt{PEGMatcher}
described in Chapter 28. The matcher consumes a grammar and an input
text and applies a stream of arms --- each binding a rule (or a
wildcard, or a channel filter) to a callback --- in ordered-choice
fashion. The matcher is the natural consumer of a grammar when the
caller wants per-rule control over which actions fire, as opposed to the
actions installed on the rules themselves.

The relationship between the two layers is simple: the definition owns
the rules and their patterns; the matcher owns the arms and their
callbacks. A rule's installed semantic action fires during
\texttt{PEGDefinition:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}};
a matcher arm's callback fires during
\texttt{PEGMatcher:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}operator\textless{}\allowbreak{}\textless{}\allowbreak{}}.
The two are independent, and a grammar may be used with either or both.

Chapter 28 covers the matcher, the \texttt{PEGMatch} record from the
matcher's perspective, channel filtering, and the wildcard arm. This
chapter has covered the definition and its rules; the matcher is the
next step for readers who need finer-grained control over match dispatch
than the installed actions provide.

\section{A Complete Tokenizer
Example}\label{ch27-a-complete-tokenizer-example}

The following example assembles the chapter's material into a complete,
self-contained tokenizer. It defines a grammar for a tiny expression
language with keywords, identifiers, numbers, operators, and
parentheses, ignores whitespace, collects tokens into a vector, and
prints them.

\begin{code}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct Token { std::string kind; std::string text; std::size_t line; };

int
main ()
{
  std::vector<Token> tokens;
  auto emit = [&](std::string kind) {
      return [&tokens, kind = std::move (kind)](dsl::PEGMatch &m) {
          tokens.push_back (Token{ kind, std::string (m.value), m.line });
      };
  };

  auto grammar = dsl::create_peg_definition ();

  auto &ws = grammar.add_rule<"[ \\t\\n\\r]+">([](dsl::PEGMatch &) {});
  ws.channel = dsl::PEGIgnoreChannel;

  grammar.add_rule<"if">   (emit ("kw_if"));
  grammar.add_rule<"then"> (emit ("kw_then"));
  grammar.add_rule<"else"> (emit ("kw_else"));
  grammar.add_rule<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*"> (emit ("ident"));
  grammar.add_rule<"[0-9]+">                 (emit ("number"));
  grammar.add_rule<"+"> (emit ("plus"));
  grammar.add_rule<"-"> (emit ("minus"));
  grammar.add_rule<"*"> (emit ("star"));
  grammar.add_rule<"/"> (emit ("slash"));
  grammar.add_rule<"("> (emit ("lparen"));
  grammar.add_rule<")"> (emit ("rparen"));

  auto r = grammar.parse ("if alpha + 3 then beta else 7");
  if (r.failed ())
    {
      std::cout << "parse failed at line " << r.error_line ()
                << " col " << r.error_column () << ": "
                << r.error_message () << "\n";
      return 1;
    }

  for (const auto &t : tokens)
    std::cout << t.line << ": " << t.kind << " '" << t.text << "'\n";
}
\end{code}

The grammar is ordered from most specific to least specific: keywords
before identifiers, single-character operators before any broader class.
The whitespace rule is first and routed to \texttt{@IGNORE}, so it
consumes inter-token spacing without producing a token. The
\texttt{emit} helper binds a kind string into each action, so each rule
produces a token with the appropriate tag.

Running this program on
\texttt{"if alpha + 3 th\allowbreak{}en beta else 7"} produces a token
list: \texttt{kw\_\allowbreak{}if},
\texttt{ident(\allowbreak{}alpha)\allowbreak{}}, \texttt{plus},
\texttt{number(\allowbreak{}3)\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{kw\_\allowbreak{}then},
\texttt{ident(\allowbreak{}beta)\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{kw\_\allowbreak{}else},
\texttt{number(\allowbreak{}7)\allowbreak{}}. The line numbers are all
\texttt{1} because the input is a single line. The whitespace between
tokens is consumed but does not appear in the vector, because the
whitespace rule is on the ignore channel and its action is a no-op.

\section{Summary}\label{ch27-summary}

The PEG definition grammar is a lightweight, additive engine for
tokenising and small-scanner tasks. A \texttt{PEGDefinition}, created
with
\texttt{create\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}definition(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}},
holds an ordered set of \texttt{PEGRule} values, each carrying a
compile-time pattern (a \texttt{FixedString} NTTP in the Chapter 9 regex
subset), a semantic action of signature
\texttt{void(\allowbreak{}PEGMatch\&)\allowbreak{}}, and a
\texttt{PEGChannel}. Rules are added with
\texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule\textless{}\allowbreak{}Pattern\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}action)\allowbreak{}},
which returns a reference for post-insertion configuration. The
\texttt{parse(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} entry point runs ordered
choice over the flattened rule list: the first matching rule at each
position wins, its action fires, and the position advances. Channels
categorise or suppress matches: \texttt{PEGDefaultChanne\allowbreak{}l}
for ordinary tokens, \texttt{PEGIgnoreChannel\allowbreak{}} for
whitespace and comments, and custom channels created with
\texttt{new\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}channel} for caller-defined
categories. Derived grammars, created with
\texttt{derive\_\allowbreak{}peg\_\allowbreak{}definition(\allowbreak{}parent)\allowbreak{}},
extend a parent without mutating it. The \texttt{PEGParseResult} reports
success or failure with offset, line, column, and a human-readable
message. Rule ordering is paramount: specific rules must precede general
ones, longer literals must precede shorter prefixes, and zero-width
matches are rejected to prevent infinite loops. The engine integrates
with the \texttt{PEGMatcher} of Chapter 28, which provides stream-style
dispatch over the same rule set, and with the AST facilities of Chapters
10 and 11 for callers who construct trees from their semantic actions.
